  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: d01fd2d64a9249049aaa77d5c12a82fe, type: 3}
  m_Name: MCQ_005
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::MCQQuestionData
  questionText: Which of the following is true about the aqueous solution of potash
    alum?
  referenceImage: {fileID: 0}
  explanationText: "Potash alum dissociates in water to produce K<sup>+</sup>, Al<sup>3+</sup>
    and SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2\u2212</sup> ions. Al<sup>3+</sup> gets attracted towards
    the lone pair of electrons present on the oxygen in water molecules and thus
    undergoes hydrolysis. Al<sup>3+</sup> + 6H<sub>2</sub>O \u21CC [Al(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3+</sup>;
    [Al(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>3+</sup> + H<sub>2</sub>O \u21CC [Al(H<sub>2</sub>O)<sub>5</sub>(OH)]<sup>2+</sup>
    + H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>. It is due to the hydrolysis of Al<sup>3+</sup>
    ions that H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup> ions are produced, and hence, the aqueous
    solution of potash alum is acidic. Thus, it can turn blue litmus red."
  options:
  - It turns red litmus blue
  - It turns blue litmus red
  - It has no effect on litmus paper
  - It is neutral in nature
  correctOptionIndex: 1
